= Answer Key — Compound Sentences (Practice 7–11)
= *First Steps in Academic Writing*, Chapter 2, pp. 54–61

== PRACTICE 8A — Identify Simple and Compound Sentences (items 1–10)

#table(
  columns: 3,
  table.header[*\#*][*Simple or Compound?*][*Formula*],
  [1], [simple], [SV],
  [2], [compound], [SV, so SSV],
  [3], [simple], [SVV],
  [4], [compound], [SV, or SVV],
  [5], [simple], [SV],
  [6], [compound], [SV, and SV],
  [7], [compound], [SV, but SV],
  [8], [compound], [SV, but SV],
  [9], [simple], [SSV],
  [10], [simple], [(S) V V],
)

*Notes:*

- Item 4: compound — second clause has compound verb "sat and told" (SVV). Comma added before "or."
- Item 6: compound — comma added before "and." Second clause has its own subject ("our father").
- Item 7: compound — comma added before "but." Second clause has its own subject ("they").
- Item 8: compound — comma added before "but." Second clause has its own subject ("we").
- Item 9: simple with compound subject "our mother and father" (SSV).
- Item 10: imperative. Implied subject "(you)" with compound verb "come" and "leave." Formula: (S) V V.

== PRACTICE 8B — Identify Simple and Compound Sentences (Teenagers, items 1–9)

#table(
  columns: 3,
  table.header[*\#*][*Simple or Compound?*][*Formula*],
  [1], [simple], [SV],
  [2], [compound], [SV, or SV],
  [3], [simple], [SVV],
  [4], [simple], [SV],
  [5], [compound], [SV, but SV],
  [6], [compound], [SV, but SV],
  [7], [simple], [SV],
  [8], [compound], [SV, so SV],
  [9], [compound], [SV, but SV],
)

*Notes:*

- Sentence 2: "First, they dye their hair purple, or they shave their heads bald." — compound, comma before "or."
- Sentence 3: "They also tattoo their skin and wear rings in their noses." — simple, SVV. One subject "they" with compound verb "tattoo and wear."
- Sentence 4: single verb "spend." The "and" joins prepositional phrases ("at the shopping mall and on the phone"), not clauses. Simple, SV.
- Sentence 7: "Finally they are old enough to drive but too young to pay for gas." — one subject "they," one linking verb "are," compound predicate adjective joined by "but." Simple, SV.
- Seven commas total across the paragraph.

== PRACTICE 9 — And, But, Or, and So (items 1–13)

#table(
  columns: 3,
  table.header[*\#*][*Coordinator*][*Explanation*],
  [1], [and], [joins two nouns in a list],
  [2], [and], [similar ideas in compound sentence],
  [3], [or], [negative — use "or" not "and"],
  [4], [but], [contrast between two ideas],
  [5], [and / but], ["and" joins nouns; "but" joins clauses (contrast)],
  [6], [so], [reason — result],
  [7], [or / but], ["or" after negative; "but" joins clauses (contrast)],
  [8], [so], [reason — result],
  [9], [and], [joins two verbs in simple sentence (compound verb)],
  [10], [so], [reason — result],
  [11], [or / so], ["or" after negative; "so" joins clauses],
  [12], [or], [alternative / choice],
  [13], [but], [contrast],
)

*Notes:*

- Item 5: first blank is within a simple sentence (joins two nouns), second blank joins two independent clauses.
- Items 3, 7, 11: in negative statements, use "or" (not "and") to join nouns or short phrases.
- Item 9: "came and brought" — simple sentence with compound verb. No comma needed.
- Item 12: "chocolate cake or apple pie" — within a simple sentence, no comma.

== PRACTICE 10A — Writing Compound Sentences (model answers)

#table(
  columns: 2,
  table.header[*\#*][*Model Answer*],
  [2], [There are several hundred languages in the world, but not all of them have a written form.],
  [3], [Chinese is spoken by more people, but English is spoken in more countries.],
  [4], [Russian is the third most spoken language in the world, and Spanish is the fourth.],
  [5], [There are about one million words in English, but most people use only about ten thousand of them.],
  [6], [Chinese has many different dialects, so Chinese people cannot always understand each other.],
  [7], [French used to be the language of international diplomacy, but now it is English.],
  [8], [International companies are growing, so they will soon need more bilingual workers.],
  [9], [Young people should know a second language, or they will be at a disadvantage in the international job market.],
)

*Alternatives:* Items 2–3 also accept "and."

== PRACTICE 11 — Fixing Run-ons and Comma Splices (items 1–10)

#table(
  columns: 3,
  table.header[*\#*][*Error?*][*Correction (Method 1 — period)*],
  [1], [X], [Some people like cats. Others prefer dogs.],
  [2], [X], [Kittens are cute. They like to play.],
  [3], [--], [Correct as is.],
  [4], [X], [It's acceptable for dogs to bark at strangers. They shouldn't bite them, however.],
  [5], [--], [Correct as is.],
  [6], [X], [Penguins always wear tuxedos. They are good pets for people who like to go to fancy parties.],
  [7], [X], [A pet elephant can fan you with his ears and spray you with his trunk. You won't need air-conditioning or a shower.],
  [8], [--], [Correct as is.],
  [9], [X], [A giraffe can reach things on high shelves. It can see over the heads of people at parades.],
  [10], [X], [Keep a boa constrictor as a pet if you enjoy being alone. Then no one will ever visit you.],
)

*Method 2 (comma + coordinator) alternatives:*

- Item 1: "...cats, and others prefer dogs." / "...cats, but others prefer dogs."
- Item 2: "Kittens are cute, and they like to play."
- Item 4: "...strangers, but they shouldn't bite them."
- Item 6: "...tuxedos, so they are good pets..."
- Item 7: "...trunk, so you won't need..."
- Item 9: "...shelves, and it can see..."
- Item 10: "...alone, and then no one will ever visit you."

*Error types:*

- Comma splices: items 1, 2, 6, 7, 9
- Run-ons: items 4, 10
- Correct: items 3, 5, 8

*Item 4:* The run-on is at "strangers they." The word "however" at the end is a conjunctive adverb, not a coordinator.
*Item 10:* "alone then" — run-on. "Then" is not a coordinator.
